1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
Some are huge.

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:04,000
This is like riding a dragon.

3
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Some are tiny.

4
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:08,000
You're inches above the ground.

5
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Can't beat that feeling.

6
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:12,000
There's nothing average about them.

7
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
You drive like people, everyone's smiling.

8
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
They're powered by the ton or by the ounce.

9
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
They can fly a whole town or a single thrill seeker.

10
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
And what happens when they go head to head?

11
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Just wait and see.

12
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:33,000
Now, it's Big and Small on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
We're used to a world populated by machines of conventional size.

14
00:00:50,001 --> 00:00:53,000
But this isn't about them.

15
00:00:53,001 --> 00:01:00,000
This is about the super big and the super small.

16
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:04,000
Take the world's largest commercial jetliner,

17
00:01:04,001 --> 00:01:07,000
the Airbus A380.

18
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
At about 240 feet long, 260 feet wide,

19
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:20,000
and 80 feet tall to the tip of the tail,

20
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:27,000
The A380 has 40% more floor space than a Boeing 747.

21
00:01:27,001 --> 00:01:31,000
That's because it's got two full cabin decks.

22
00:01:31,001 --> 00:01:39,000
And depending on the interior design, it can carry anywhere from 450 to 850 passengers at a time,

23
00:01:39,001 --> 00:01:43,000
for flights of more than 15 hours.

24
00:01:43,001 --> 00:01:49,000
A bigger plane with more passengers also means a heavier plane.

25
00:01:49,001 --> 00:01:50,680
We've tested the aircraft.

26
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:53,760
A take-off weight is over 1.3 million pounds.

27
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:56,960
That's the highest take-off weight of any commercial airplane.

28
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:01,480
Getting that much weight off the ground requires wings with a surface area

29
00:02:01,481 --> 00:02:04,720
about the size of two professional basketball courts.

30
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,320
The size of the wings on the A380,

31
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,960
and about double the area of the wing of a 747,

32
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:13,560
some of that comes from the increased width.

33
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:17,320
You have a wing area advantage and a wingspan advantage for take-off.

34
00:02:17,321 --> 00:02:20,580
And what we can do with that airplane is really quite astounding.

35
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:25,740
You can take off at that weight and you can climb direct to 35,000 feet in 20 minutes.

36
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:29,920
You do it with an engine that's only about 10% bigger than the engine on a 747.

37
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:34,600
Climbing higher faster means less noise around the airport.

38
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:39,100
It also gets the aircraft to a more fuel-efficient altitude quicker.

39
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:44,520
But there's a lot more to this superjumbo than big wings and those slightly bigger engines.

40
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:51,940
Bringing all the elements together is a task that's, well, gigantic.

41
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:58,640
We're currently standing in the A380 final assembly building, the Jean-Luc Lagadier building in Toulouse,

42
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:01,700
which is one of the assembly sites that Airbus uses.

43
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:05,260
You could put ten soccer fields under this roof.

44
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:13,780
But before any of the parts get here, Airbus pre-assembles the largest components

45
00:03:13,781 --> 00:03:16,640
at four other major facilities across Europe.

46
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:24,320
Hamburg, Germany supplies the front and rear cabin sections.

47
00:03:24,321 --> 00:03:29,320
Saint-Nazaire, France provides the forward and center fuselage.

48
00:03:29,321 --> 00:03:32,320
Broughton Whale sends the full wings.

49
00:03:32,321 --> 00:03:36,320
And Puerto Real Spain the horizontal tail.

50
00:03:36,321 --> 00:03:41,320
If only bringing them together were just that easy.

51
00:03:41,321 --> 00:03:46,320
Transporting these mammoth components is no small task.

52
00:03:46,321 --> 00:03:52,200
The 380 fuselage is bigger than any other aircraft fuselage, so it just doesn't fit in an aeroplane.

53
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:55,860
For Airbus, that meant specially built transport ships,

54
00:03:56,140 --> 00:04:01,020
with extra large cargo bays to deliver the components to western France.

55
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:08,900
From there, giant barges transport each piece downriver, bypassing major city streets.

56
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,200
Finally, the parts hit the road.

57
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:17,800
Special A380 trucks tow them south nearly 150 miles.

58
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:22,740
They only travel at night at a maximum speed of 18 miles per hour.

59
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,220
After roughly three nights, they reach Toulouse.

60
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:33,320
What we do here is we take the fuselage sections that arrive from the other parts of Europe,

61
00:04:33,321 --> 00:04:36,960
we bring them into the facility, and then we actually start building an airplane.

62
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:42,260
So we take the three fuselage sections and we put those into an assembly jig.

63
00:04:42,261 --> 00:04:45,640
From there, we then add the two wings and the tail plane.

64
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:51,660
In about a week, a plane is on its wheels and can be moved to other parts of the facility.

65
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:59,200
Where we can then start building in the engines, the other equipment that we need to get the airplane up to a flying state.

66
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:02,280
And normally, a whole process for about five or six weeks.

67
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:09,520
To make this giant as efficient and safe as possible, Airbus performs regular flight tests from Toulouse.

68
00:05:09,521 --> 00:05:15,500
We're on the main deck of A380, model serial number 001.

69
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:19,380
This is the first A380 ever produced, and it's our premier test aircraft.

70
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:24,760
Airbus utilizes this enormous plane to test the A380 under various flight conditions.

71
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:29,520
When we're doing flight tests, we work as a team.

72
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:35,440
We have the pilots in the front, and the flight engineer sits back here at this very high-tech console,

73
00:05:35,441 --> 00:05:38,980
and he monitors and records the data that we're collecting,

74
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:41,920
he can monitor the flight and assist us as necessary

75
00:05:41,921 --> 00:05:44,040
if we want to move on to the next flight condition.

76
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:47,880
While they conduct studies with the original A380,

77
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:51,000
pilots must also test each assembled plane

78
00:05:51,001 --> 00:05:52,820
before the aircraft can leave Toulouse.

79
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:57,260
The tested aircraft will then fly back to Homburg to be furnished

80
00:05:57,261 --> 00:05:59,360
and get a paint job.

81
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:05,160
There's an extra benefit of having over 47,000 square feet

82
00:06:05,161 --> 00:06:09,975
surface area to paint. The 380 is the world's largest flying billboard.

83
00:06:09,976 --> 00:06:11,060
They have 10,000

84
00:06:11,061 --> 00:06:13,666
square feet of space that the airlines can use.

85
00:06:13,667 --> 00:06:15,940
The aircraft actually spends a week being

86
00:06:15,941 --> 00:06:19,069
prepared and painted with a number of different layers of the paint.

87
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:20,220
The final one, of course,

88
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:22,466
being the airline color scheme.

89
00:06:22,467 --> 00:06:26,980
About 1,400 pounds of paint later, the A380 is ready for action.

90
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:34,460
The layout of the A380 is determined by the purchaser, but Airbus supplies the airlines

91
00:06:34,461 --> 00:06:36,620
with a few suggestions in their mock-up center.

92
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:43,760
While the A380 is surprisingly 7 feet shorter

93
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:45,360
than a standard Boeing 747,

94
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:48,180
it's 20 inches wider.

95
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:52,800
With the extra 20 inches, it means that across 10 seats

96
00:06:52,801 --> 00:06:55,060
we can put a little bit of extra space into the aisleway,

97
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:57,540
but also about 1 1⁄2 inches extra width

98
00:06:57,541 --> 00:06:59,320
in the cabin seat itself here in Coach.

99
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,720
Coach isn't the only class to benefit from more space.

100
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:10,682
This is an A380 super suite, so I think it's time to spend some relaxation time.

101
00:07:10,683 --> 00:07:11,460
Excuse me.

102
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:20,460
While customers may enjoy the creature comforts, the pilots still have to land this giant on a runway.

103
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:29,460
Airbus designed the A380's 22 wheels to help disperse the plane's immense weight more evenly across the surface.

104
00:07:29,461 --> 00:07:34,628
the surface. The airports don't have to go out and strengthen their runways.

105
00:07:34,629 --> 00:07:35,920
Once on the ground,

106
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,764
the designers have given pilots a little extra help taxiing this big plane.

107
00:07:40,765 --> 00:07:41,780
All the avionics

108
00:07:41,781 --> 00:07:43,294
have been very well designed.

109
00:07:43,295 --> 00:07:46,740
We've put a taxi camera on the airplane so that you can see a view

110
00:07:46,741 --> 00:07:51,400
from the tail and see where your landing gear is, and you can also see where your landing gear is

111
00:07:51,401 --> 00:07:56,816
going to go. So it really improves your ability to move the airplane on the ground.

112
00:07:56,817 --> 00:07:57,860
At the terminal,

113
00:07:57,861 --> 00:08:00,760
the key is to get passengers on and off in a timely manner.

114
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:05,040
We have three doors at the front of the aircraft,

115
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:06,760
two on the main deck and one on the upper deck,

116
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:09,100
and that means that we can get the passenger flow

117
00:08:09,101 --> 00:08:11,240
through the aircraft very, very quickly.

118
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:14,560
To keep the turnaround time to about an hour and a half,

119
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:17,380
airports load food directly to the upper deck,

120
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:19,620
and some have extended their baggage belts.

121
00:08:21,060 --> 00:08:23,920
The 380s covers a lot of issues with a lot of people.

122
00:08:23,921 --> 00:08:28,700
It can be the high-comfort, high-end airplane for the traditional airlines,

123
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:34,000
and also the high-capacity aircraft for those operators that need a high-capacity, low-cost airplane.

124
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:41,320
If the Airbus A380 is the largest commercial jet, what's its counterpart in the world of the big and small?

125
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:50,840
The Marana Northwest Regional Airport near Tucson, Arizona, is home to Aerial Productions International and their tiny jets.

126
00:08:53,920 --> 00:08:58,080
This aircraft is the world's smallest jet.

127
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:01,680
We refer to it as a microjet, or the military refers to it as a Smart One.

128
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:05,360
It's a small, manned, aerial radar target, Model 1.

129
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:09,940
And this airplane is only 17 foot wide, 12 feet long.

130
00:09:10,560 --> 00:09:15,180
It's about 4 1⁄2 feet high at the top, and it weighs about 500 pounds.

131
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:19,540
And yet it's capable of speeds of up to 300 miles an hour.

132
00:09:19,541 --> 00:09:27,360
to us it's the indy race car of the air but people look at this and they say my god that's

133
00:09:27,361 --> 00:09:34,820
smaller than a volkswagen and it is in fact over 15 of these tiny jets could line up across the a380s

134
00:09:34,821 --> 00:09:40,760
wingspan as many as 20 could follow one another from nose to tail

135
00:09:40,761 --> 00:09:46,040
so what kind of engine powers this bitty baby

136
00:09:46,041 --> 00:09:51,420
The actual engine, which sits back in the back part of this airplane, is only about

137
00:09:51,421 --> 00:09:53,867
12 inches in diameter and two feet long.

138
00:09:53,868 --> 00:09:56,680
And yet it puts out enough power to power this

139
00:09:56,681 --> 00:09:59,023
airplane up to speeds of 300 miles an hour.

140
00:09:59,024 --> 00:10:01,420
This engine is probably the world's smallest

141
00:10:01,421 --> 00:10:03,054
manned aircraft engine.

142
00:10:03,055 --> 00:10:07,600
Smaller by far than the power generators on larger aircraft that

143
00:10:07,601 --> 00:10:12,520
just generated electricity. It's very tiny.

144
00:10:12,521 --> 00:10:21,280
So small, in fact, nearly 700 could fit inside a single A380 engine housing.

145
00:10:21,281 --> 00:10:27,680
Also known as the BD-5J, this little plane was originally developed in the 1970s by the

146
00:10:27,681 --> 00:10:29,280
BD Aircraft Corporation.

147
00:10:29,281 --> 00:10:34,680
And it was supposed to be a little home-built, put-it-together-yourself airplane, originally

148
00:10:34,681 --> 00:10:39,220
driven by a propeller configuration with a snowmobile engine in it, a little snowmobile

149
00:10:39,221 --> 00:10:40,220
engine.

150
00:10:40,221 --> 00:10:42,260
It later became a jet, and the jet was the key.

151
00:10:43,020 --> 00:10:45,380
This airplane intrigued everybody because of its speed.

152
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:48,160
It looked like it was doing 1,000 miles an hour because it's so tiny.

153
00:10:50,140 --> 00:10:51,720
Apparently, James Bond liked it.

154
00:10:52,480 --> 00:10:53,920
He flew one in Octopussy.

155
00:10:55,420 --> 00:11:00,560
For Bob Bishop, flying BD-5Js has been a passion for over 30 years.

156
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:08,600
For nine of them, he did stunts on a little jet better known as the Coors Silver Bolt.

157
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:14,860
Today, his Smart One microjets have a new job,

158
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:17,220
this time with the military.

159
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:23,540
The Smart One's involved right now in different types of radar targeting use for the military.

160
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:27,140
They substitute for cruise missiles, among other things.

161
00:11:28,540 --> 00:11:31,740
We're just what they call low observable target, hard to see.

162
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:34,340
It's hard to see on radar, it's hard to see in the air.

163
00:11:34,341 --> 00:11:40,240
So we can fly places that they couldn't fly an unmanned vehicle.

164
00:11:40,380 --> 00:11:43,540
And also the fact that they can talk to us and we can change course immediately.

165
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:46,260
I'm going to do a left 360.

166
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:47,940
So we're very flexible.

167
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:52,000
Now with all the special electronics we have aboard, James Bond would be very proud.

168
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:59,000
For this airplane, tall pilots need not apply for the job because you have to be less than 5'10".

169
00:11:59,860 --> 00:12:01,700
You're actually wearing the airplane.

170
00:12:01,701 --> 00:12:06,700
It's a real nice fit, and they do have size limits and weight limits on us,

171
00:12:06,701 --> 00:12:09,400
so it encourages me to stay trim on that.

172
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:14,440
But I'm about the max on the height. I'm 5'9", and that's about the maximum size.

173
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:18,800
There's another maximum, no shoe size over 11.

174
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:23,740
The pilot's feet fit right up to the nose of the plane

175
00:12:23,741 --> 00:12:26,840
and must be able to easily maneuver the rudder pedals.

176
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:31,220
These pedals help with ground steering and braking.

177
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:36,700
In the air, maneuvering the jet is in the palm of the pilot's hand.

178
00:12:36,701 --> 00:12:38,057
It's a side stick control.

179
00:12:38,058 --> 00:12:42,700
You simply control the airplane like a game controller, so kids would relate to this one.

180
00:12:42,701 --> 00:12:46,854
And you simply kind of think left and it goes left. It's that sensitive.

181
00:12:46,855 --> 00:12:48,700
And it's just a delight in life.

182
00:12:48,701 --> 00:12:54,700
You have to take your clothes off to have more fun than this. It's a lot of fun.

183
00:12:54,701 --> 00:13:03,080
For those who are a bit too big to fit in this small cockpit, maybe something on two

184
00:13:03,081 --> 00:13:10,860
wheels with a 450-horsepower engine will do.

185
00:13:10,861 --> 00:13:14,860
Jets may have their immensely big and impressively small representatives.

186
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:19,260
But down on the ground, so do motorcycles.

187
00:13:19,261 --> 00:13:23,320
Let's start with the big.

188
00:13:23,321 --> 00:13:25,580
You might think of these as large.

189
00:13:25,581 --> 00:13:26,580
Think again.

190
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:31,320
Meet the Boss Hoss,

191
00:13:31,321 --> 00:13:33,940
biggest production motorcycle in the world.

192
00:13:33,941 --> 00:13:36,500
Your typical production cruiser motorcycle

193
00:13:36,501 --> 00:13:38,940
is probably gonna have, on the high end,

194
00:13:38,941 --> 00:13:43,940
around 100 horsepower, and will weigh 800, 900 pounds,

195
00:13:43,941 --> 00:13:47,580
where conversely, the Boss Hoss weighs on the low end

196
00:13:47,581 --> 00:13:51,640
around 1,100, can be up to about 1,300 pounds.

197
00:13:51,641 --> 00:13:52,760
What's that extra weight about?

198
00:13:53,720 --> 00:13:57,560
This, a V8 engine intended to muscle an automobile.

199
00:14:00,340 --> 00:14:03,840
Boss Haas was created in the winter of 89-90

200
00:14:03,841 --> 00:14:07,740
when Monty Warren took his 350 Chevrolet car engine

201
00:14:07,741 --> 00:14:12,880
out of his stock car and crafted a frame around that engine

202
00:14:12,881 --> 00:14:15,560
and produced the first Boss Haas motorcycle.

203
00:14:15,561 --> 00:14:25,000
Today, Boss Haas includes a line of two- and three-wheeled motorcycles, all manufactured

204
00:14:25,001 --> 00:14:28,500
in Dyersburg, Tennessee.

205
00:14:28,501 --> 00:14:33,100
Just like the first one, every Boss Haas cycle begins with the engine.

206
00:14:33,101 --> 00:14:35,960
The Chevy V8 engine is the heart of the Boss Haas.

207
00:14:35,961 --> 00:14:41,160
We'll get the engine in and install our own componentry to the engine.

208
00:14:41,161 --> 00:14:44,860
In order to handle the extra size and weight of the engine,

209
00:14:44,861 --> 00:14:47,460
Boss Haas custom-built its own 1 1⁄2-inch

210
00:14:47,461 --> 00:14:50,860
chrome MOLLE tube frames.

211
00:14:50,861 --> 00:14:53,260
Workers fit the frame to the engine

212
00:14:53,261 --> 00:14:57,560
and add a two-speed transmission.

213
00:14:57,561 --> 00:14:59,620
Then steering, wiring, and other components

214
00:14:59,621 --> 00:15:03,160
go on to complete each bike.

215
00:15:03,161 --> 00:15:05,960
Finally, it's off to a dealership, where many of them

216
00:15:05,961 --> 00:15:09,260
will receive custom paint jobs.

217
00:15:09,261 --> 00:15:12,260
You're pretty hard pressed to find any two Boss Haases that look alike.

218
00:15:13,260 --> 00:15:16,540
The standard 350 horsepower engine is still not big enough.

219
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:20,580
I'm about to take a ride on a Boss Haase motorcycle,

220
00:15:20,700 --> 00:15:22,720
the most powerful production bike on the planet,

221
00:15:22,721 --> 00:15:25,000
and this one has a Corvette engine.

222
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:26,180
Holy cow.

223
00:15:26,181 --> 00:15:39,560
445 foot-pounds of torque, all directed to the rear tire.

224
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:41,340
Unbelievable.

225
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:45,060
445 horsepower on a motorcycle.

226
00:15:45,960 --> 00:15:49,040
This big bike averages about 20 miles per gallon.

227
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:54,960
And its large engine demands a wheelbase over 12 inches longer than many motorcycles.

228
00:15:56,180 --> 00:16:04,960
It's probably one of the easiest riding motorcycles when you get past the intimidation factor of the size of the Boss Hoss.

229
00:16:06,740 --> 00:16:11,280
It weighs 1,100 pounds. It rides like it's 600 pounds.

230
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,860
Just no telling what the top end is on this thing.

231
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:19,860
A few riders have gone in excess of 200 miles per hour.

232
00:16:21,660 --> 00:16:23,740
But no one has dared to max it out.

233
00:16:23,741 --> 00:16:28,140
I think this thing goes from zero to wet your pants in about two seconds.

234
00:16:29,080 --> 00:16:30,440
This is like riding a dragon.

235
00:16:32,040 --> 00:16:35,600
The large engine isn't the only automotive-inspired part of this beast.

236
00:16:37,340 --> 00:16:42,920
A unique distinguishing feature of the Boss Haas is this large automotive-style radiator

237
00:16:42,921 --> 00:16:44,440
that's out front of the engine.

238
00:16:44,860 --> 00:16:50,100
Because we're utilizing the car engine, we need a larger-than-normal cooling capacity

239
00:16:50,101 --> 00:16:51,640
for a motorcycle.

240
00:16:51,641 --> 00:16:56,640
The Monster has been designed to fit riders of all sizes.

241
00:16:56,641 --> 00:17:03,020
This bike can be ridden comfortably by an individual that's five foot tall or an individual

242
00:17:03,021 --> 00:17:04,900
that's seven foot tall.

243
00:17:04,901 --> 00:17:09,400
Just by adjusting the foot peg position will help give the rider a comfortable feel.

244
00:17:09,401 --> 00:17:14,000
But with a large bike comes a large price tag.

245
00:17:14,001 --> 00:17:21,600
The base price starts at $40,000 and the LS3, the Corvette engine multiport fuel injected

246
00:17:21,601 --> 00:17:27,780
boss hoss which is our big boy they start around fifty thousand dollars

247
00:17:27,781 --> 00:17:34,600
about one-thirtieth the weight of a boss hoss and one hundredth the horsepower

248
00:17:34,601 --> 00:17:40,460
comes the smallest production motorcycle designed to fit almost anyone's budget

249
00:17:40,461 --> 00:17:44,460
how do they match up in a race

250
00:17:51,600 --> 00:18:01,140
Well, we're not too surprised.

251
00:18:01,141 --> 00:18:06,160
Pocket bikes, also known as mini-motos, are generally about two feet long and one and

252
00:18:06,161 --> 00:18:07,560
a half feet tall.

253
00:18:07,561 --> 00:18:11,980
They're a little motorcycle that can fit in the backseat of an import car.

254
00:18:11,981 --> 00:18:13,900
They weigh about 40 pounds.

255
00:18:13,901 --> 00:18:17,080
Top speed on a really nice one is about 60 miles an hour.

256
00:18:17,081 --> 00:18:20,940
You're able to do everything that you can on a real motorcycle, but you're just a lot

257
00:18:20,941 --> 00:18:21,740
closer to the ground.

258
00:18:26,380 --> 00:18:29,040
While they can't beat a boss hoss off the line,

259
00:18:29,880 --> 00:18:32,720
enthusiasts have been seriously racing them in Asia and Europe

260
00:18:32,721 --> 00:18:34,060
since the 1990s.

261
00:18:37,860 --> 00:18:40,240
Today in Minimoto, racing is gaining momentum

262
00:18:40,241 --> 00:18:43,560
in the United States, in spite of laws restricting

263
00:18:43,561 --> 00:18:44,660
their use on streets.

264
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:50,280
Clubs like Pocket Bike GP in Central California

265
00:18:50,281 --> 00:18:54,280
Use go-kart tracks or parking lots to race their pocket rockets.

266
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:59,280
Some of the best riders in the world started out this way.

267
00:18:59,281 --> 00:19:04,280
Valentino Rossi being one of them, and he won the MotoGP last year in superbikes,

268
00:19:04,281 --> 00:19:06,280
and he started in pocket bikes.

269
00:19:06,281 --> 00:19:09,280
Riding a pocket bike is identical to riding a full-size bike.

270
00:19:09,281 --> 00:19:13,280
It's all the same finesse, all the same maneuvers.

271
00:19:15,280 --> 00:19:19,280
Because you're lower to the ground, the sensation, 30, feels like 130.

272
00:19:19,281 --> 00:19:20,640
I don't even road race anymore.

273
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:21,780
I just do this.

274
00:19:22,460 --> 00:19:25,360
I can crash five times and drive myself home.

275
00:19:27,820 --> 00:19:32,140
Many of its basic components are miniaturized versions of standard race motorcycles.

276
00:19:33,320 --> 00:19:36,040
Here's your kind of standard pocket bike.

277
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:39,880
All the body work has been removed to show all the components.

278
00:19:40,980 --> 00:19:45,800
It has pneumatic slick tires, so you're able to do the incredible lean angles

279
00:19:45,801 --> 00:19:47,560
and have really good traction,

280
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:50,280
which is important when you're this close to the ground.

281
00:19:51,320 --> 00:19:53,360
It has front and back disc brakes,

282
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:57,500
so you're able to stop very easily with hardly any effort.

283
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:01,100
The basic engines that come on pocket bikes

284
00:20:01,101 --> 00:20:04,460
are similar to stuff that you would find in yard equipment,

285
00:20:04,880 --> 00:20:09,100
like a weed eater or something in your garage.

286
00:20:09,600 --> 00:20:10,400
They're two-strokes.

287
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:12,980
They have centrifugal clutch, so it's one speed.

288
00:20:13,100 --> 00:20:14,220
You just give it gas and go.

289
00:20:15,800 --> 00:20:18,540
Pocket bikes range from 2 to 18 horsepower.

290
00:20:19,940 --> 00:20:21,980
They can also cost as much as $5,000.

291
00:20:23,720 --> 00:20:26,600
For that, you can get better parts, a stronger frame,

292
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:28,300
and a lot more speed.

293
00:20:30,740 --> 00:20:33,100
This is a higher-end bike I purchased and used.

294
00:20:33,580 --> 00:20:35,340
It's a Polini 6.2.

295
00:20:36,440 --> 00:20:39,440
And this is probably between 11 and 12 horsepower,

296
00:20:39,820 --> 00:20:41,240
which, I mean, it doesn't sound like much,

297
00:20:41,320 --> 00:20:42,560
but when you think how much it weighs,

298
00:20:42,660 --> 00:20:44,600
it's mind-boggling how fast it is.

299
00:20:45,800 --> 00:20:51,800
While the motorcycle may be small in size, you don't have to be small to ride one.

300
00:20:51,801 --> 00:20:56,800
I'm about 6'3 and about 220 pounds. The pocket bike comes just below my knee.

301
00:20:56,801 --> 00:21:03,800
So for me the biggest challenge is actually getting my feet to a place where I can not drag them on the ground and wear through my shoes as much.

302
00:21:03,801 --> 00:21:08,800
So typically what you do is you put your foot right there and you kind of wedge it in between the frame there and you get like this.

303
00:21:08,801 --> 00:21:12,800
And you adjust your knee pads so that way when you go in the turn you can get your nice tripod effect.

304
00:21:15,800 --> 00:21:21,520
For someone who doesn't know pocket bikes, they look like toys, but you know, you just

305
00:21:21,521 --> 00:21:24,680
get on when you do a couple hot laps, you'll change your mind real fast.

306
00:21:24,681 --> 00:21:31,080
You've never ridden a pocket bike, you can't fully appreciate the intensity of it.

307
00:21:31,081 --> 00:21:35,280
So have we covered the world of big and small motorcycles?

308
00:21:35,281 --> 00:21:36,280
Not quite.

309
00:21:36,281 --> 00:21:42,700
While these speedy mini-motos may be the smallest production motorcycles, and the Boss Haas

310
00:21:42,701 --> 00:21:45,700
the largest.

311
00:21:45,701 --> 00:21:50,380
would be crushed by the Dream Big, the world's biggest

312
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:53,640
rideable motorcycle.

313
00:21:53,740 --> 00:21:57,840
Created by Gregory Dunham of Stockton, California,

314
00:21:57,940 --> 00:22:01,080
this monster 500 horsepower two-wheeler

315
00:22:01,180 --> 00:22:08,260
measures in at 11 feet 3 inches tall and 20 feet 4 inches long.

316
00:22:08,360 --> 00:22:10,560
The tires alone are over 6 feet in diameter.

317
00:22:10,561 --> 00:22:16,800
The Dream Big also weighs a staggering 6,500 pounds,

318
00:22:16,801 --> 00:22:18,800
about the same as a large SUV.

319
00:22:18,801 --> 00:22:23,800
It's capable of reaching 65 miles per hour,

320
00:22:23,801 --> 00:22:27,320
burning through gas at a rate of 6 miles per gallon.

321
00:22:27,321 --> 00:22:31,420
Not exactly the best commuter vehicle.

322
00:22:31,421 --> 00:22:36,060
For most of us, driving to work involves a four-wheeler,

323
00:22:36,061 --> 00:22:37,920
known as an automobile.

324
00:22:37,921 --> 00:22:39,860
Some are gigantic.

325
00:22:39,861 --> 00:22:43,280
gigantic and some roll away like a piece of luggage.

326
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:49,340
A car says a lot about the person who drives it.

327
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:53,000
So can its size.

328
00:22:53,980 --> 00:22:56,560
Small says efficient and environmentally friendly.

329
00:22:57,880 --> 00:23:01,100
And while cars like these are definitely small by today's standards,

330
00:23:02,140 --> 00:23:04,760
they are giants in the microcar kingdom.

331
00:23:09,860 --> 00:23:12,960
So how many microcars can one man own?

332
00:23:13,900 --> 00:23:15,180
How about 300?

333
00:23:17,920 --> 00:23:20,180
After making his money in bubble gum,

334
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:23,760
Bruce Weiner put some of that cash toward creating

335
00:23:23,761 --> 00:23:27,340
the world's largest collection of the world's smallest cars.

336
00:23:29,540 --> 00:23:31,980
The Bruce Weiner Microcar Museum

337
00:23:31,981 --> 00:23:35,220
pays tribute to their nearly forgotten little world.

338
00:23:36,260 --> 00:23:39,020
A microcar would be described as a car

339
00:23:39,021 --> 00:23:42,120
that's under 700cc engine displacement.

340
00:23:42,121 --> 00:23:44,480
It would be a three wheel or four wheel car.

341
00:23:44,481 --> 00:23:46,620
It would be born after World War II,

342
00:23:46,621 --> 00:23:50,140
and it can have a full top, a convertible top, or no top.

343
00:23:50,141 --> 00:23:52,260
And it can seat anywhere from two to four people.

344
00:23:52,261 --> 00:23:54,240
We also have some single seaters,

345
00:23:54,241 --> 00:23:56,580
but generally speaking, they're two-seater cars.

346
00:23:56,581 --> 00:23:57,840
At the center of this collection

347
00:23:57,841 --> 00:24:01,240
sits some of the smallest of the microcars,

348
00:24:01,241 --> 00:24:03,600
including the Peel P50,

349
00:24:03,601 --> 00:24:05,560
the smallest production car ever built.

350
00:24:05,561 --> 00:24:09,560
It was built on the Isle of Man in the 50s.

351
00:24:09,561 --> 00:24:11,560
They only built about 15 of them.

352
00:24:11,561 --> 00:24:13,560
There's only 14 known to exist.

353
00:24:13,561 --> 00:24:17,560
I'm 6'1", and you can see where it comes up to my chest.

354
00:24:17,561 --> 00:24:19,560
So it's quite small.

355
00:24:19,561 --> 00:24:21,560
It has an engine size of about 50 cc's,

356
00:24:21,561 --> 00:24:24,560
and horsepower of about 7 or 8 horsepower.

357
00:24:24,561 --> 00:24:27,560
Travels about 30 miles an hour, top speed.

358
00:24:28,560 --> 00:24:30,560
It's about 150, 200 pounds.

359
00:24:30,561 --> 00:24:31,560
Very, very light.

360
00:24:31,561 --> 00:24:33,560
It does not have reverse.

361
00:24:33,561 --> 00:24:35,260
So when you park it, if you want to move it,

362
00:24:35,360 --> 00:24:38,140
you go to the back of it, and it has a handle on the back,

363
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:40,900
and you pick it up with one arm, and you move it around

364
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,800
to put it in a parking spot or take it out of a parking spot.

365
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:47,200
It's actually quite comfortable, as small as it looks.

366
00:24:47,300 --> 00:24:50,140
You get in from the rear side first.

367
00:24:50,240 --> 00:24:51,240
Put yourself inside.

368
00:24:54,880 --> 00:24:57,420
It also easily fits in the back of a pickup truck.

369
00:24:59,620 --> 00:25:01,320
But those who prefer to bring a friend

370
00:25:01,321 --> 00:25:05,080
a friend may enjoy the somewhat space-age-looking heel trident.

371
00:25:09,220 --> 00:25:13,300
Or perhaps they'd like to open up the front of a car and just take a seat.

372
00:25:16,780 --> 00:25:18,840
Some microcars even fit a family.

373
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:25,040
Microcars were especially appealing to European countries trying to recover from the war.

374
00:25:26,800 --> 00:25:31,300
Back then, these were selling for $200 and $300, and in America, cars here were $15.

375
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:33,320
to $2,000 up to $3,000.

376
00:25:33,321 --> 00:25:35,320
So they were relatively inexpensive.

377
00:25:35,321 --> 00:25:37,320
They were also inexpensive to operate.

378
00:25:37,321 --> 00:25:41,320
Three-wheeled microcars came with an extra benefit.

379
00:25:41,321 --> 00:25:44,320
Back then they had a road tax on automobiles,

380
00:25:44,321 --> 00:25:46,320
and if it was a three-wheeler, it was considered a motorcycle

381
00:25:46,321 --> 00:25:48,320
and there wasn't a road tax put on them.

382
00:25:48,321 --> 00:25:51,320
If they were 50 cc's or under, it didn't require a driver's license.

383
00:25:51,321 --> 00:25:54,320
And most people in Europe didn't have driver's license.

384
00:25:56,320 --> 00:26:00,320
Many microcars benefited from their two-wheeled cousins in other ways as well.

385
00:26:01,320 --> 00:26:03,620
When they started building them, a lot of common parts were from scooters.

386
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:05,400
A lot of these take scooter tires.

387
00:26:05,780 --> 00:26:07,600
Most of them use scooter engines.

388
00:26:09,220 --> 00:26:14,160
But some of the most successful microcars were designed by aircraft engineers and manufacturers

389
00:26:14,161 --> 00:26:16,620
interested in civilian projects.

390
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,980
The holy grail of microcars are Messerschmitts.

391
00:26:21,220 --> 00:26:23,780
The reason is because they were the best engineered.

392
00:26:25,700 --> 00:26:28,480
The inventor of the Messerschmitt, Fend Flitzer,

393
00:26:28,481 --> 00:26:33,620
created his first automobile as a method of transportation for war amputees.

394
00:26:34,220 --> 00:26:40,400
He built this for a person in a wheelchair that basically didn't have his legs

395
00:26:40,401 --> 00:26:42,160
because there aren't any pedals in the car.

396
00:26:42,420 --> 00:26:47,140
This car has a handbrake and a clutch, and you would move this to change the gears.

397
00:26:47,141 --> 00:26:50,420
And from this, it evolved into the Messerschmitt, what we know today.

398
00:26:50,780 --> 00:26:54,800
They built around 9,000 cars over the period of the total production.

399
00:26:54,801 --> 00:27:00,460
By the mid-1960s, the microcar era came to a close,

400
00:27:01,060 --> 00:27:03,840
to impart to consumer demands for greater creature comforts

401
00:27:03,841 --> 00:27:05,600
and increased safety standards.

402
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:11,000
Although these impish small cars might not seem practical today,

403
00:27:11,660 --> 00:27:13,020
they've still got a lure.

404
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:18,060
They're just so much fun to drive and to be around and to have,

405
00:27:18,160 --> 00:27:20,460
because when you meet people, you drive by people, everyone's smiling.

406
00:27:21,580 --> 00:27:23,020
It brings the best out of people.

407
00:27:23,021 --> 00:27:27,700
Like the microcars, the big boys have also had their days in the sun.

408
00:27:28,880 --> 00:27:36,140
The late 1920s and early 1930s gave us the Grand Bugatti Royale and the Duesenberg SJ,

409
00:27:36,900 --> 00:27:38,920
two of the largest cars ever produced.

410
00:27:41,060 --> 00:27:46,960
By the early 1970s, immense Cadillacs and Lincolns became the ultimate symbol of success.

411
00:27:47,820 --> 00:27:52,760
They comfortably seated seven, and some Cadillacs had the biggest V8 engine of the time.

412
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:56,300
An 8.2 liter, 400 horsepower monster.

413
00:27:59,260 --> 00:28:04,820
30 years later, the three-ton gas-guzzling Hummers came to be roadhog champs.

414
00:28:07,300 --> 00:28:10,940
This Hummer easily towers over a small fleet of microcars.

415
00:28:12,900 --> 00:28:17,860
But if a Hummer just isn't big enough, you can always stretch it.

416
00:28:17,861 --> 00:28:27,340
That's the kind companies like exotic VIP limos in Southern California are banking on.

417
00:28:27,341 --> 00:28:33,420
When it comes to limos and super stretchers, this will be as big as it gets inside of a

418
00:28:33,421 --> 00:28:39,140
Hummer X2 limo and you have a luxury of everything and anything you want to have inside a limo,

419
00:28:39,141 --> 00:28:40,140
period.

420
00:28:40,141 --> 00:28:47,740
At 40 feet long, the H2 limo has enough room for 12 PLP 50 microcars to take

421
00:28:47,741 --> 00:28:48,441
a ride.

422
00:28:48,442 --> 00:28:54,920
That may seem impressive, but Exotic VIP likely has something else in mind for

423
00:28:54,921 --> 00:28:57,540
its big limo.

424
00:28:57,541 --> 00:29:02,980
It carries 30 people comfortably with every single thing that they want and ask for in

425
00:29:02,981 --> 00:29:03,980
their hands.

426
00:29:03,981 --> 00:29:08,980
That's important when you're transporting a big group, like the Granada Hills Charter

427
00:29:08,981 --> 00:29:15,500
High School Choir. Even this monster mover has its limitations.

428
00:29:15,501 --> 00:29:21,380
We can only haul up to 15,000 pounds with the passenger, with fuel and everything inside.

429
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:28,880
But before this heavy limo can carry that load, it had to be transformed from a comparably lighter Hummer, like this.

430
00:29:30,320 --> 00:29:35,240
Original Hummers, when you buy the car from the factory, it weighs anywhere from 5,000 to 6,000 pounds.

431
00:29:35,640 --> 00:29:39,860
However, when we do stretch it out, it's anywhere from 8,000 to 9,000 pounds.

432
00:29:39,861 --> 00:29:49,260
Super stretching any vehicle requires cutting it in half and extending the entire body and adding some heavy-duty support.

433
00:29:50,540 --> 00:29:54,220
It also means adding plenty of seating to accommodate an entire choir.

434
00:29:57,100 --> 00:30:02,640
And when it comes to these super stretch limos, the original Hummer muscle is up to the task.

435
00:30:02,641 --> 00:30:06,960
One of the main reasons that we can super-sweep this SUV into a limousine,

436
00:30:07,460 --> 00:30:11,100
that's because the engine and the transmission is pretty heavy-duty,

437
00:30:11,280 --> 00:30:13,340
and it can support the weight. It can speed up.

438
00:30:13,520 --> 00:30:15,620
It can take off easily with no problem whatsoever.

439
00:30:16,400 --> 00:30:18,720
The big challenge comes with a sharp turn.

440
00:30:19,840 --> 00:30:23,240
They don't bend from the middle, so it's pretty hard to imagine when you're driving,

441
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:25,840
especially on traffic and small, narrow roads.

442
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:28,280
When it's a white street, no problem.

443
00:30:28,600 --> 00:30:29,880
There's a car parking there.

444
00:30:29,881 --> 00:30:33,720
that's it, you have to go back and forth about two or three times.

445
00:30:35,220 --> 00:30:38,480
Hallelujah! Hallelujah!

446
00:30:39,260 --> 00:30:41,940
While the driving may be a bit tricky around the corners,

447
00:30:42,360 --> 00:30:44,800
that's a small price to pay for passenger satisfaction.

448
00:30:44,801 --> 00:30:49,320
Hallelujah! Hallelujah! Hallelujah!

449
00:30:50,960 --> 00:30:53,760
This hefty automobile is a big hit with the choir.

450
00:30:54,520 --> 00:30:57,180
Hallelujah! Hallelujah! Hallelujah!

451
00:30:57,181 --> 00:31:06,180
But it'll take a tire heavier than the entire limo to help drive one monster machine.

452
00:31:06,181 --> 00:31:13,180
There's big, and then there's mind-boggling big.

453
00:31:13,181 --> 00:31:20,180
This is the 994F with an overall length of 55 feet.

454
00:31:20,181 --> 00:31:27,180
It is 19 feet wide, 22 feet high, it weighs 450,000 pounds.

455
00:31:27,181 --> 00:31:34,180
This colossal front loader weighs over 50 times more than a Ford F-250 pickup.

456
00:31:34,181 --> 00:31:41,180
The tires on the 994F weigh 10,000 pounds alone.

457
00:31:41,181 --> 00:31:49,180
They are 13 feet high, 48 inches wide, at a cost of right at $50,000.

458
00:31:49,181 --> 00:31:53,480
As you can see, we have chains on this 994.

459
00:31:53,481 --> 00:31:56,300
We work in a limestone base here.

460
00:31:56,301 --> 00:32:01,860
Without these chains, we could ruin a tire at any time by cutting them.

461
00:32:01,861 --> 00:32:04,460
The chains weigh 10,000 pounds.

462
00:32:04,461 --> 00:32:08,940
We're expecting to run these chains for 23,000 hours.

463
00:32:08,941 --> 00:32:12,560
That is approximately three sets of tires.

464
00:32:12,561 --> 00:32:18,940
The home for this Caterpillar 994F is Oxford Mining's open pit coal mine outside of Caddis,

465
00:32:18,941 --> 00:32:20,940
Ohio.

466
00:32:20,941 --> 00:32:24,980
Owning a big loader like this comes with a big price tag,

467
00:32:24,981 --> 00:32:28,120
about $4.5 million.

468
00:32:28,121 --> 00:32:30,320
It's quite an investment, but over the time,

469
00:32:30,321 --> 00:32:33,780
it is a major piece of equipment.

470
00:32:33,781 --> 00:32:35,800
It is our major stripper.

